WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.100
Listing all the ways Avengers: Endgame made
us emotional would take forever.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.550
Instead, we'll focus on just one spoiler-ridden
aspect of the superhero ensemble that packed

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.879
a heart-rending punch: the line little Morgan
Stark told her father, Tony, early on in the

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:16.250
film, and the same one he repeated back to
her at the end.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.250
Reportedly, Avengers: Endgame directors Joe
and Anthony Russo sat down with Watching Hollywood

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.360
and explained the origins of the adorable
and impactful line.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.230
Translated to English by the folks at ComicBook.com,
the interview details that the "I love you

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:31.689
3,000" line came about from Tony Stark actor
Robert Downey Jr.

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:35.250
The actor told the Russo brothers that one
of his three children told him they loved

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.550
him that much, and the directors found the
story so sweet that they couldn't resist incorporating

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.320
the line into Endgame twice.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.890
Since this explanation is said to have come
straight from the people in charge, it effectively

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.750
shuts down all other speculation fans stirred
up around the "I love you 3,000" line.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.390
Some theorized that the phrase Morgan told
her father may have been a clever comeback

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.220
to him mentioning "tons" when he told her
how much he loved her.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.910
One U.S. ton equals 2,000 pounds — and 3,000
is a bit more than that figure.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:05.410
Another idea Marvelites had was that the "3,000"
of the phrase is actually a nod to the Marvel

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:10.170
Cinematic Universe, as 3,000 is the combined
number of minutes every already-released movie

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.090
and the yet-to-launch Spider-Man: Far From
Home run for.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.890
Figures from IMDb pin the MCU thus far — from
Iron Man to Avengers: Endgame — as running

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.130
for a total of 2,867 minutes.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.509
Assuming that Spider-Man: Far From Home will
match the 133-minute runtime of Spider-Man:

00:01:25.509 --> 00:01:27.860
Homecoming, that brings the number up to 3,000.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.060
"I love you 3,000" was one of two unforgettable
lines Tony Stark gave in Avengers: Endgame.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.240
The other came at the climax.

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:38.200
After Tony retrieves the Infinity Stones from
Thanos and locks them into his own suit, the

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.860
Mad Titan says, "I am inevitable."

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.060
Thanos snaps his fingers, then looks shocked
to see Tony wielding the Infinity Stones.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.900
Just as Thanos' line mirrors what he told
the surviving Avengers at the start of Endgame,

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.480
Tony gives an equally memorable callback when
he says "I am Iron Man."

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:53.240
"The truth is…

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:01.460
I am Iron Man."

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.060
While "I love you 3,000" was apparently thought
out in advance, "I am Iron Man" was a snap

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:06.560
decision.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.900
The Russo brothers admitted during a Q&amp;A session
for Avengers: Endgame that Tony's powerful

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.640
moment in the film wasn't included in the
script — and didn't even make it into the

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.660
movie until the filmmakers were editing.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.300
According to the Russos:

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.700
"Tony used to not say anything in that moment.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.600
And we were in the editing room going, 'He
has to say something.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.830
This a character who has lived and died by
quips.'

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.660
And we just couldn't, we tried a million different
last lines.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.300
Thanos was saying, 'I am inevitable.'

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.720
And our editor Jeff Ford […] said, 'Why
don't we just go full circle with it and say

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:34.720
I am Iron Man?'"

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.250
Tony Stark began the MCU as we know it with
Iron Man, and he ended the franchise's 11-year

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:40.950
saga with Avengers: Endgame.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.100
His character arc finally came to an emotional
close when he bravely sacrificed his life

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.730
to ensure the safety of the entire universe.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.530
It's only right that he was the messenger
of some of the hardest-hitting lines in the

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:54.830
film, and the hero who likely made audiences
openly weep in their theater seats.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.660
Though he's gone for good, it's clear that
fans will always love him…

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.160
3,000.

